.Dd November 10, 2026
.Dt INPUT 4
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm input
.Nd the event devices of the keyboards, mice, tablets and buttons
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In linux/input.h
.Pp
.Pa /dev/input/event0
to
.Pa /dev/input/event31
.Sh DESCRIPTION
Each input device the kernel drives is a node of
.Pa /dev/input ,
with Linux's evdev interface: major 13, minor 64 and up.
A node is listed only while its device is there, and a device takes the
lowest free node when it is found.
Each device and its node are also listed under
.Pa /sys/class/input ,
below the PS/2 port or USB interface the device came from, and a uevent
announces each as it comes and goes, as udev and libinput expect; see
.Xr sysfs 5 .
The PS/2 keyboard is
.Dq AT Translated Set 2 keyboard ,
as
.Dv EVIOCGNAME
gives it.
A keyboard's keys reach its node beside the console, which every keyboard
shares; see
.Xr console 4 .
So do the keys of any other device that can report a key below
.Dv BTN_MISC
or a Braille key, as Linux's console takes them: the power and sleep
buttons, a hotkey device, a keypad.
.Pp
Each human-interface interface of a USB device is read by its report
descriptor, as Linux's usbhid and hid-generic read it, and has a node for
each application of the descriptor, a System Control or Consumer Control
application sharing a keyboard's; a keyboard whose media keys are a second
interface has nodes for both.
Its name is its manufacturer's and product's strings, a space between, or
.Dq HID VVVV:PPPP ,
its vendor and product in hexadecimal, when it has neither; a device of more
than one application adds the application's name to each node's, as
.Dq Keyboard ,
.Dq Mouse
or
.Dq Consumer Control ,
unless the name ends with it.
Its bus is 3, its version bcdHID from its HID descriptor, and its place
.Dq usb-N/inputI ,
I the interface's number.
Its capabilities and events are those Linux gives the same descriptor: each
key and button is reported with its usage as
.Dv MSC_SCAN ,
a wheel with
.Dv REL_WHEEL_HI_RES
in 120ths of a detent, and an absolute axis over its logical range.
QEMU's
.Cm usb-tablet
is
.Dq QEMU QEMU USB Tablet ,
with
.Dv BTN_LEFT
to
.Dv BTN_EXTRA ,
.Dv REL_WHEEL ,
.Dv REL_WHEEL_HI_RES ,
and
.Dv ABS_X
and
.Dv ABS_Y
from 0 to 32767.
A keyboard or mouse whose descriptor cannot be read runs in the boot
protocol, as
.Dq USB HIDBP Keyboard VVVV:PPPP
or
.Dq USB HIDBP Mouse VVVV:PPPP
when it has no strings, as Linux's usbkbd and usbmouse name it.
.Pp
The PS/2 mouse is named for the protocol it answers, as Linux's psmouse
names it: a mouse that answers Microsoft's IntelliMouse Explorer knock is
.Dq ImExPS/2 Generic Explorer Mouse ,
product 6, with a wheel, a horizontal wheel and five buttons; one that
answers the IntelliMouse's alone is
.Dq ImPS/2 Generic Wheel Mouse ,
product 5, with a wheel and three buttons; any other is
.Dq PS/2 Generic Mouse ,
product 1, with three buttons.
Each is vendor 2 on the PS/2 controller's bus, at
.Dq isa0060/serio1/input0 ,
with the property
.Dv INPUT_PROP_POINTER .
A USB mouse has the buttons, axes and wheel its descriptor names and no
property.
Every mouse with relative axes also feeds
.Pa /dev/mouse ,
whose three-byte packets carry no wheel; a tablet does not.
.Pp
A Synaptics touchpad on the PS/2 port, which answers the Synaptics knock
before the mouse's, is set up as Linux's psmouse sets it: absolute mode, 80
packets a second, W mode, and the advanced gesture mode where it has one.
It is
.Dq SynPS/2 Synaptics TouchPad ,
product 7 and vendor 2 on the PS/2 controller's bus, at
.Dq isa0060/serio1/input0 ,
its version the model's sensor and low byte, 0x01b1 on the Lenovo's.
Its capabilities are those Linux gives the same pad:
.Dv ABS_X
and
.Dv ABS_Y
with the limits and resolution the pad gives,
.Dv ABS_PRESSURE
to 255,
.Dv ABS_TOOL_WIDTH
to 15 on a pad that measures it,
.Dv BTN_TOUCH ,
.Dv BTN_TOOL_FINGER
and, on a pad that counts fingers,
.Dv BTN_TOOL_DOUBLETAP
and
.Dv BTN_TOOL_TRIPLETAP ,
.Dv BTN_LEFT
and
.Dv BTN_RIGHT ,
.Dv BTN_MIDDLE
with
.Dv BTN_FORWARD
and
.Dv BTN_BACK
on a pad with a middle button,
.Dv BTN_0
and up for its extra buttons, and
.Dv INPUT_PROP_POINTER .
A clickpad, whose pad is its button, has
.Dv BTN_LEFT
alone and
.Dv INPUT_PROP_BUTTONPAD .
A pad with an image sensor, as the Lenovo's is, keeps two slots:
.Dv ABS_MT_SLOT ,
.Dv ABS_MT_TRACKING_ID ,
.Dv ABS_MT_POSITION_X
and
.Dv ABS_MT_POSITION_Y
as
.Dv ABS_X
and
.Dv ABS_Y ,
.Dv ABS_MT_PRESSURE ,
and
.Dv BTN_TOOL_QUADTAP
and
.Dv BTN_TOOL_QUINTTAP ;
a pad with the advanced gesture mode and no image sensor keeps two slots
that bound its two contacts, with
.Dv INPUT_PROP_SEMI_MT .
A Synaptics pad that answers the knock but not a query or the mode is set
back to relative mode and named for the mouse protocol it answers,
.Dq PS/2 Synaptics TouchPad
and the like.
.Pp
What PS/2 does not tell, Linux 6.12 takes from the ACPI PNP IDs of the PS/2
mouse, which are its serio port's
.Pa firmware_id ,
.Ql PNP: LEN0036 PNP0f13
and the like, and so does the kernel.
The ThinkPads Linux's
.Va min_max_pnpid_table
names get the
.Dv ABS_X
and
.Dv ABS_Y
limits it sets in place of what their pads report.
A clickpad whose ID is in Linux's
.Va topbuttonpad_pnp_ids ,
and which has no stick's buttons wired to it, has
.Dv INPUT_PROP_TOPBUTTONPAD .
A ForcePad, SYN300D or SYN3014, gives
.Dv BTN_LEFT
only for one finger pressed for more than 50 ms, until it lifts, since it
gives its click for more fingers too.
A pad that offers SMBus stays on PS/2, as Linux's does without its SMBus
driver, and the log says whether Linux built with one would move it there.
.Pp
A pad with a pass-through port, as a ThinkPad's is, carries the packets of
the device behind it, which is found as Linux's psmouse finds one there:
its ID, a TrackPoint's read-ID command, and the IntelliMouse knocks.
A TrackPoint is
.Dq TPPS/2 IBM TrackPoint ,
or
.Dq ALPS ,
.Dq Elan ,
.Dq NXP ,
.Dq JYT_Synaptics
or
.Dq Synaptics
in place of
.Dq IBM
as its kind is, product 10 and vendor 2 on the PS/2 controller's bus, at
.Dq synaptics-pt/serio0/input0 ,
with
.Dv BTN_LEFT ,
.Dv BTN_RIGHT ,
.Dv BTN_MIDDLE
when it has three buttons,
.Dv REL_X ,
.Dv REL_Y ,
.Dv INPUT_PROP_POINTER
and
.Dv INPUT_PROP_POINTING_STICK .
It is set up as Linux sets it: an IBM stick is reset to its power-on
settings, and any other stick, or one whose reset fails, is given Linux's.
Its settings are files of its serio port, which
.Xr sysfs 5
lists: each reads as a number, and root writes a number from 0 to 255, or
for a switch a truth as Linux's
.Fn kstrtobool
reads it, which the stick takes at once.
The buttons of a stick that the pad wires to itself are reported on the
stick's node when they change.
.Pp
A TrackPoint alone on the auxiliary port, which answers its read-ID when
no touchpad answered, is found, named and set up the same way, at
.Dq isa0060/serio1/input0 ,
and its settings are files of serio1.
.Pp
A mouse behind the port is named and numbered as the PS/2 mouse above, at
.Dq synaptics-pt/serio0/input0 .
.Pp
An ALPS touchpad, which answers the ALPS reports when no Synaptics pad has
answered, is identified and set up as Linux's alps driver does it: an old
model by its signature in Linux's table, protocol versions 1 and 2 and
version 6, and a newer one by its firmware's report, versions 3 to 5, 7
and 8.
A version 9 pad is left to the mouse driver, as Linux leaves it, and ALPS's
CS19, a stick with no pad, to the TrackPoint driver, as Linux leaves it.
The pad is
.Dq AlpsPS/2 ALPS GlidePoint ,
or
.Dq AlpsPS/2 ALPS DualPoint TouchPad
on a model with a pointing stick, product 8 and vendor 2 on the PS/2
controller's bus, at
.Dq isa0060/serio1/input0 ,
its version the protocol's, 0x100 to 0x800, 0x310 for version 3 in its
Rushmore form.
On versions 1, 2 and 6 it reports one finger,
.Dv ABS_X
and
.Dv ABS_Y
to the pad's limits and
.Dv ABS_PRESSURE
to 127, with
.Dv REL_WHEEL ,
.Dv BTN_FORWARD
and
.Dv BTN_BACK ,
or
.Dv BTN_0
to
.Dv BTN_3
on the models that have them.
Versions 3 to 5 keep four slots that bound the contacts, with
.Dv INPUT_PROP_SEMI_MT
and
.Dv ABS_PRESSURE ;
versions 7 and 8 keep four slots that follow the contacts at any distance,
with
.Dv BTN_TOOL_QUINTTAP ,
and
.Dv ABS_PRESSURE
on version 8 alone.
No ALPS pad reports
.Dv ABS_MT_PRESSURE .
Every pad has
.Dv BTN_TOUCH ,
.Dv BTN_TOOL_FINGER ,
.Dv BTN_LEFT
and
.Dv INPUT_PROP_POINTER ;
a clickpad has no other button and has
.Dv INPUT_PROP_BUTTONPAD .
The stick is
.Dq AlpsPS/2 ALPS DualPoint Stick ,
product 8 and the pad's version, at
.Dq isa0060/serio1/input1 ,
with
.Dv REL_X ,
.Dv REL_Y ,
.Dv BTN_LEFT ,
.Dv BTN_RIGHT
and
.Dv BTN_MIDDLE ,
.Dv ABS_PRESSURE
to 127 on versions 3, 7 and 8, and the properties
.Dv INPUT_PROP_POINTER
and
.Dv INPUT_PROP_POINTING_STICK .
A mouse on the pad's external port is
.Dq PS/2 ALPS Mouse ,
product 1 and version 0, at
.Dq isa0060/serio1/input2 ,
or input1 on a pad with no stick; its first packet registers it and is
dropped.
The stick and the external mouse feed
.Pa /dev/mouse
as a mouse does.
A version 6 pad's buttons are reported on the stick's device, as Linux
reports them, and that device's packet ends with the pad's.
A pad that refuses its set-up is driven as a mouse, named for the mouse
protocol it answers,
.Dq PS/2 ALPS GlidePoint
and the like.
.Pp
An Elantech touchpad of hardware version 1 to 4, which answers Elantech's
knock where Synaptics' knock and ALPS's reports found nothing, is set up
as Linux's elantech driver sets it: its firmware and capabilities queried,
its registers written for absolute mode, 100 packets a second.
It is
.Dq ETPS/2 Elantech Touchpad ,
product 14 and vendor 2 on the PS/2 controller's bus, at
.Dq isa0060/serio1/input0 ,
version 0, with the capabilities Linux gives the same pad:
.Dv ABS_X
and
.Dv ABS_Y
with the pad's range, and on version 4 its resolution,
.Dv BTN_TOUCH ,
.Dv BTN_TOOL_FINGER ,
.Dv BTN_TOOL_DOUBLETAP
and
.Dv BTN_TOOL_TRIPLETAP ,
.Dv BTN_LEFT
and
.Dv BTN_RIGHT ,
.Dv BTN_MIDDLE
on a pad Linux knows to have one, and
.Dv INPUT_PROP_POINTER .
Version 1 adds
.Dv BTN_FORWARD
and
.Dv BTN_BACK
on a pad with a rocker.
Versions 2 and 3 keep two slots that bound two contacts, with
.Dv INPUT_PROP_SEMI_MT ,
.Dv ABS_PRESSURE
to 255 and
.Dv ABS_TOOL_WIDTH
to 15 where the pad reports pressure, and version 2
.Dv BTN_TOOL_QUADTAP .
Version 4 keeps five slots with
.Dv ABS_MT_PRESSURE
and
.Dv ABS_MT_TOUCH_MAJOR ,
and
.Dv BTN_TOOL_QUADTAP .
A clickpad of version 3 or 4 has
.Dv BTN_LEFT
alone and
.Dv INPUT_PROP_BUTTONPAD .
A pointing stick on a version 3 or 4 pad is a node of its own, registered
before the pad's,
.Dq ETPS/2 Elantech TrackPoint ,
product 14 at
.Dq isa0060/serio1/input1 ,
with three buttons,
.Dv REL_X ,
.Dv REL_Y ,
.Dv INPUT_PROP_POINTER
and
.Dv INPUT_PROP_POINTING_STICK ;
it also moves the pointer of
.Pa /dev/mouse .
A pad that answers the knock but not a query or a register, or a version 3
pad whose absolute mode Linux does not use, is reset and named for the
mouse protocol it answers,
.Dq PS/2 Elantech Touchpad
and the like.
.Pp
After five packets in a row that break their form, a pad's that it
refuses or a mouse's first byte without its framing bit, or the bytes
0xAA 0x00 that a device sends when it has reset itself, the kernel thread
kpsmoused reconnects it as Linux's psmouse does: a Synaptics pad is
reset, must give the identity, model and capabilities it gave before, and
is set up again, then the device behind its port; an Elantech pad is
reset, must answer Elantech's knock again, and is put back in absolute
mode; an ALPS pad is reset, must again be one Linux drives, and is set up
again; a mouse is reset and must answer with the protocol it spoke before;
and their nodes stay.
A pad or a mouse that does not answer, or is another one, is probed anew,
its nodes removed and registered again; so is a device behind the port
that no longer answers as it did.
.Pp
The ACPI devices of
.Xr acpi 4
are nodes too:
.Dq Power Button ,
.Dq Sleep Button ,
.Dq Video Bus
and, on an IdeaPad,
.Dq Ideapad extra buttons
of
.Xr ideapad 4 ,
on the host's bus,
.Dv BUS_HOST .
Each reports a key pressed and then released at once, in two packets, with
no
.Dv MSC_SCAN ,
since the firmware's event has no key code of its own.
.Pp
A
.Xr read 2
gives whole
.Vt struct input_event
records, as many as fit in its count: the time of the event, its type, its
code and its value.
The time is two
.Vt long Ns s ,
seconds and microseconds, so a record is 16 bytes on i686 and 24 on
x86-64, as
.In linux/input.h
lays it out under the C library's 64-bit
.Vt time_t .
An i386 program on the x86-64 kernel reads records of 16 bytes, and the
bit sets of
.Dv EVIOCGBIT
and its kin in 32-bit
.Vt long Ns s ,
as Linux gives them
.Pq Xr ia32 7 .
Every record of a packet has the time of the packet's first event, read on
the clock the open chose with
.Dv EVIOCSCLOCKID :
the real time unless it chose another, as on Linux.
.Pp
A key is reported as one packet of three records:
.Dv EV_MSC
.Dv MSC_SCAN
with the code the keyboard sent,
.Dv EV_KEY
with Linux's key code,
.Dv KEY_A
to
.Dv KEY_MAX ,
and the value 1 for a press and 0 for a release, and
.Dv EV_SYN
.Dv SYN_REPORT ,
which ends the packet.
The PS/2 keyboard's
.Dv MSC_SCAN
is its set-1 code with the release bit clear and 0x80 set after an E0
prefix, as Linux's keyboard driver gives a translated keyboard's: 0x1e for
A, 0xb0 for Volume Up.
A USB keyboard's is the key's usage in the keyboard page, 0x70004 for A.
A key with no key code gives the
.Dv MSC_SCAN
alone.
.Pp
The keyboards repeat in software, every keyboard alike: the last key
pressed that is not a modifier or a lock repeats 250 ms after its press and
then every 33 ms, Linux's times, until it is released.
A repeat is a packet of two records,
.Dv EV_KEY
with the value 2 and
.Dv EV_SYN
.Dv SYN_REPORT .
A press of a key that is down already, which a PS/2 keyboard sends while
the key is held, is dropped.
.Pp
A mouse's packet is its buttons that changed,
.Dv EV_KEY
.Dv BTN_LEFT
to
.Dv BTN_EXTRA
with 1 for a press and 0 for a release; its motion,
.Dv EV_REL
.Dv REL_X
to the right and
.Dv REL_Y
toward the user; a wheel's turn,
.Dv REL_WHEEL
in detents, up positive, and
.Dv REL_HWHEEL ,
right positive; and
.Dv SYN_REPORT .
A USB mouse reports each turn again in 120ths of a detent,
.Dv REL_WHEEL_HI_RES ,
as Linux's generic HID driver does.
.Pp
A touchpad's packet is Linux's for the same pad.
On a pad with slots, each contact is
.Dv ABS_MT_SLOT
when the slot changes,
.Dv ABS_MT_TRACKING_ID ,
a new number for a new contact and \-1 for a lifted one, and its
.Dv ABS_MT_POSITION_X ,
.Dv ABS_MT_POSITION_Y
and
.Dv ABS_MT_PRESSURE ;
a contact keeps its slot while it moves less than 10 mm between packets.
Then come the pointer of the oldest contact,
.Dv BTN_TOUCH ,
.Dv ABS_X ,
.Dv ABS_Y
and
.Dv ABS_PRESSURE ;
the number of fingers the pad counted, one of
.Dv BTN_TOOL_FINGER
to
.Dv BTN_TOOL_QUINTTAP ;
the buttons; and
.Dv SYN_REPORT .
Y grows toward the user.
The touchpad moves the pointer of
.Pa /dev/mouse
as Linux's mousedev moves that of
.Pa /dev/input/mice :
while a finger touches, from the third packet on, by the change over the
last two packets, the pad's width being 256 counts on both axes; a touch
shorter than 200 ms is a click of the left button.
.Pp
The events pass the filters of Linux's input core: an event of a key or
axis the device does not have, a press of a key that is down, a release of
one that is up, a relative motion of 0, and an absolute value within the
axis's fuzz of the last are dropped, and so is a
.Dv SYN_REPORT
that would end a packet with nothing in it.
When the repeat's times change, each keyboard reports the ones that
changed as
.Dv EV_REP
.Dv REP_DELAY
and
.Dv REP_PERIOD ,
in milliseconds; when the console's LEDs change, each keyboard reports
those that changed as
.Dv EV_LED
.Dv LED_NUML ,
.Dv LED_CAPSL
and
.Dv LED_SCROLLL ,
1 for lit; each change ends with
.Dv SYN_REPORT .
.Pp
Each open has a queue of its own, sized as Linux's evdev sizes it: eight
packets of the size the device's axes and slots suggest, at least 64
records and a power of two, which gives a keyboard 64 records, a mouse 128
and the Lenovo's touchpad 256.
A read waits until a whole packet is queued and then returns as many whole
records as its buffer holds, so a buffer smaller than the packet splits it
and the next read returns the rest, as on Linux.
A reader that falls so far behind that its queue fills loses what it had:
the queue keeps an
.Dv EV_SYN
.Dv SYN_DROPPED
and the newest record, and the reader should drop what it reads up to the
next
.Dv SYN_REPORT
and ask for the keys held with
.Dv EVIOCGKEY .
A read waits for a packet unless the descriptor is nonblocking;
.Xr poll 2
reports
.Dv POLLOUT ,
.Dv POLLIN
while a packet is queued, or
.Dv POLLHUP
with
.Dv POLLERR
once the device has gone or the open was revoked.
.Pp
.Xr write 2
takes whole records in the reader's layout, as many as fit in its count
and in 4096 bytes, and returns the bytes it took; each record's type, code
and value are given to the device as if it had reported them, and its time
is ignored, as Linux's evdev takes a write.
A type the device lacks is dropped.
The other events go through the filters above to the device's opens, the
writer's own among them, or to the open that holds the grab alone, and a
key of a device whose keys the console takes that reaches them with no grab
held also reaches the console as a key typed, and a mouse's motion and buttons reach
.Pa /dev/mouse
as a packet at each
.Dv SYN_REPORT ,
as Linux's mousedev takes them.
A keyboard's
.Dv EV_LED
of
.Dv LED_NUML ,
.Dv LED_CAPSL
or
.Dv LED_SCROLLL
lights or puts out that LED, and its
.Dv EV_REP
sets the repeat's
.Dv REP_DELAY
or
.Dv REP_PERIOD ,
of every keyboard, as the console's LEDs and repeat are shared; the
change reaches each keyboard's opens as a packet of its own.
Any other
.Dv EV_LED
the device has, such as a USB keyboard's
.Dv LED_COMPOSE
and
.Dv LED_KANA ,
lights or puts out that LED on that device alone, and reaches its opens,
as on Linux.
.Pp
The ioctls are Linux's:
.Bl -tag -width EVIOCGBIT
.It Dv EVIOCGVERSION
The interface's version, 0x010001.
.It Dv EVIOCGID
A
.Vt struct input_id :
the bus, 0x11 for the PS/2 controller and 0x03 for USB, the vendor, the
product and the version: a USB device's HID version, as Linux's usbhid
gives it, a Synaptics or ALPS touchpad's and an ALPS stick's, and 0 for
the others.
.It Dv EVIOCGNAME , EVIOCGPHYS
The device's name and its place,
.Dq isa0060/serio0/input0
for the PS/2 keyboard and
.Dq usb-N/inputI
for interface I of a USB keyboard, cut to the length the request gives, as Linux cuts
them; the answer is the bytes copied.
.It Dv EVIOCGUNIQ
The serial number of a USB device in the report protocol, its serial
string in at most 63 bytes, a character that would not fit left out, or
the empty string for a device without one, as Linux's usbhid gives it;
.Er ENOENT
for a PS/2 device and a USB keyboard or mouse in the boot protocol, as
Linux's usbkbd and usbmouse give none.
.It Dv EVIOCGBIT
The bits of the event types the device reports,
.Dv EV_SYN ,
for a device with keys or buttons
.Dv EV_KEY ,
for a mouse
.Dv EV_REL ,
for a device with absolute axes
.Dv EV_ABS ,
for a device with LEDs
.Dv EV_LED ,
and, for a keyboard,
.Dv EV_MSC ,
.Dv EV_LED
and
.Dv EV_REP ;
of the keys and buttons it can report; of its relative and absolute axes;
of its
.Dv EV_MSC
codes,
.Dv MSC_SCAN ;
and of its LEDs, for a keyboard
.Dv LED_NUML ,
.Dv LED_CAPSL
and
.Dv LED_SCROLLL
among them.
The other types answer no bits, and
.Dv EV_REP
answers
.Er EINVAL ,
as on Linux.
.It Dv EVIOCGKEY , EVIOCGLED
The bits of the keys down now, or of the LEDs lit, none for a device
without.
As on Linux, the records of that type still queued for the open are taken
out of its queue, since the state read includes them, and a packet left
empty goes with them.
.It Dv EVIOCGPROP
The bits of the device's properties:
.Dv INPUT_PROP_POINTER
for a PS/2 mouse and a touchpad, and
.Dv INPUT_PROP_BUTTONPAD
or
.Dv INPUT_PROP_SEMI_MT
for a touchpad that is one, and
.Dv INPUT_PROP_POINTING_STICK
for a pointing stick, with
.Dv INPUT_PROP_POINTER .
.It Dv EVIOCGSND , EVIOCGSW
No bits: no device here has sounds or switches.
.It Dv EVIOCGABS , EVIOCSABS
Read or set a
.Vt struct input_absinfo
of an absolute axis: its value, minimum, maximum, fuzz, flat and
resolution.
A structure shorter than Linux's sets the resolution to 0.
A device with no absolute axis answers
.Er EINVAL ,
and so does
.Dv EVIOCSABS
of
.Dv ABS_MT_SLOT .
.Dv EVIOCGABS
of
.Dv ABS_MT_SLOT
gives the slot the last event was for.
.It Dv EVIOCGMTSLOTS
Fill the
.Vt int32_t Ns s
after the code at the argument, a multitouch axis such as
.Dv ABS_MT_TRACKING_ID ,
with that axis's value in each slot, as many as the slots and the
request's length allow.
.It Dv EVIOCGRAB
With a nonzero argument, take the device for this open alone, so that no
other open reads its events until it gives it back, with 0, or closes.
A grabbed device's keys do not reach the console either, nor a grabbed
mouse's packets
.Pa /dev/mouse ,
as on Linux.
.It Dv EVIOCREVOKE
With the argument 0, end this open's use of the device for good: it gets
no more events, gives up a grab it holds, and reads, polls and asks as if
the device had gone, while the device's other opens go on.
A seat manager such as seatd revokes a session's descriptors when the
session loses the seat.
.It Dv EVIOCSCLOCKID
Stamp this open's records by the clock whose id the int argument holds:
.Dv CLOCK_REALTIME ,
the default,
.Dv CLOCK_MONOTONIC ,
which libinput chooses, or
.Dv CLOCK_BOOTTIME ,
which reads as the monotonic clock, as
.Xr clock_gettime 2
reads it.
A change of clock empties a queue that holds records and puts a
.Dv SYN_DROPPED ,
stamped by the new clock, in their place, read with the next packet.
.It Dv EVIOCGMASK , EVIOCSMASK
Read or set, through a
.Vt struct input_mask ,
the mask of one event type, a bit for each code, which leaves out of this
open's queue the codes whose bits are off; the mask of
.Dv EV_SYN
chooses whole types.
A type with no mask set reads all ones.
A code past the buffer's size is off, a type that takes no mask is
ignored, and
.Dv SYN_REPORT
always passes.
.It Dv EVIOCGREP , EVIOCSREP
Read into, or set from, two unsigned ints a keyboard's repeat: the delay
before a held key's first repeat and the period between repeats, in
milliseconds.
Every keyboard shares one repeat, which the console's
.Dv KDKBDREP
also sets; see
.Xr console 4 .
A time above
.Dv INT_MAX
is left as it is, and a period of 0 turns repeat off.
A device that is no keyboard answers
.Er ENOSYS .
.It Dv EVIOCGKEYCODE , EVIOCSKEYCODE , EVIOCGKEYCODE_V2 , EVIOCSKEYCODE_V2
Read or change the key code a scan code reports, as udev's hwdb sets
them: the legacy forms take two unsigned ints, the scan code and the key
code, and the
.Dv _V2
forms a
.Vt struct input_keymap_entry ,
by its scan code of 1, 2 or 4 bytes or, with
.Dv INPUT_KEYMAP_BY_INDEX ,
by its place in the map.
The PS/2 keyboard's map has 512 entries, indexed by the set-1 code with
0x80 set after E0, as Linux's atkbd keeps it; a USB keyboard's or other
HID device's entries are its usages, page and usage in one 32-bit code,
those of its input and output reports that are keys or nothing, as
Linux's hid-input numbers them.
A changed key becomes one the device has, and the old one stops being one
unless another scan code reports it, a press of it held released first.
.It Dv EVIOCGEFFECTS , EVIOCSFF , EVIOCRMFF
No device here has force feedback, so
.Dv EVIOCGEFFECTS
gives 0 effects and the other two answer
.Er ENOSYS ,
as on Linux.
.El
.Pp
The nodes are root's, mode 0600, as Linux's are.
BusyBox's
.Cm acpid
reads them; see
.Xr busybox-util-linux 1 .
